Text: patterns with the preposition “for” are substantially more common in the FEEL class than the FACT class. Table 4 shows examples of learned NP Prep pat- terns with the preposition “of” in the FACT class and “for” in the FEEL class. The “of” preposition in the factual arguments often attaches to objective terminology. The “for” preposition in the feeling- based arguments is commonly used to express advo- cacy (e.g., demand for) or refer to affected popula- tion groups (e.g., treatment for).

